{"tool_name":"rtn","contents":["bioconda","bioconductor","biotools","oeb"],"identifiers":["biotools:rtn","bioconductor:rtn","bioconda:bioconductor-rtn"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"rtn","home":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/RTNsurvival","license":"Artistic-2.0","summary":"RTNduals identifies co-regulatory loops between pairs of regulons inferred by the RTN package by evaluating their shared target genes. It infers dual regulons and tests whether regulator pairs exhibit cooperative or competitive influences on common targets.","addition_date":"2017-01-17T15:03:29Z","last_update_date":"2026-11-22T22:42:19.792070Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Library"],"version":["1.36.0"],"collections":["BioConductor"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:rtn","credit":[{"name":"Clarice S. Groeneveld"},{"name":"Mauro A. A. Castro"},{"name":"Vinicius S. Chagas"}],"documentation":[{"type":["User manual"],"url":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/RTNsurvival"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Pathway or network analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2497"}]}],"name":"RTN","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"bioconductor_import","publication":[{"doi":"10.1038/ng.3458","metadata":{"abstract":"Genetic risk for breast cancer is conferred by a combination of multiple variants of small effect. To better understand how risk loci might combine, we examined whether risk-associated genes share regulatory mechanisms. We created a breast cancer gene regulatory network comprising transcription factors and groups of putative target genes (regulons) and asked whether specific regulons are enriched for genes associated with risk loci via expression quantitative trait loci (eQTLs). We identified 36 overlapping regulons that were enriched for risk loci and formed a distinct cluster within the network, suggesting shared biology. The risk transcription factors driving these regulons are frequently mutated in cancer and lie in two opposing subgroups, which relate to estrogen receptor (ER)(+) luminal A or luminal B and ER(-) basal-like cancers and to different luminal epithelial cell populations in the adult mammary gland. Our network approach provides a foundation for determining the regulatory circuits governing breast cancer, to identify targets for intervention, and is transferable to other disease settings.","authors":[{"name":"Campbell TM"},{"name":"Castro MA"},{"name":"Hickey TE"},{"name":"Markowetz F"},{"name":"Meyer KB"},{"name":"Ponder BA"},{"name":"Ross E"},{"name":"Tilley WD"},{"name":"Vaughn C"},{"name":"de Santiago I"}],"citationCount":187,"date":"2016-01-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nature genetics","title":"Regulators of genetic risk of breast cancer identified by integrative network analysis."},"pmcid":"PMC4697365","pmid":"26618344"},{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btz229","metadata":{"abstract":"<h4>Motivation</h4>Transcriptional networks are models that allow the biological state of cells or tumours to be described. Such networks consist of connected regulatory units known as regulons, each comprised of a regulator and its targets. Inferring a transcriptional network can be a helpful initial step in characterizing the different phenotypes within a cohort. While the network itself provides no information on molecular differences between samples, the per-sample state of each regulon, i.e. the regulon activity, can be used for describing subtypes in a cohort. Integrating regulon activities with clinical data and outcomes would extend this characterization of differences between subtypes.<h4>Results</h4>We describe RTNsurvival, an R/Bioconductor package that calculates regulon activity profiles using transcriptional networks reconstructed by the RTN package, gene expression data, and a two-tailed Gene Set Enrichment Analysis. Given regulon activity profiles across a cohort, RTNsurvival can perform Kaplan-Meier analyses and Cox Proportional Hazards regressions, while also considering confounding variables. The Supplementary Information provides two case studies that use data from breast and liver cancer cohorts and features uni- and multivariate regulon survival analysis.<h4>Availability and implementation</h4>RTNsurvival is written in the R language, and is available from the Bioconductor project at http://bioconductor.org/packages/RTNsurvival/.<h4>Supplementary information</h4>Supplementary data are available at Bioinformatics online.","authors":[{"name":"Castro MAA"},{"name":"Chagas VS"},{"name":"Groeneveld CS"},{"name":"Jones SJM"},{"name":"Meyer KB"},{"name":"Ponder BAJ"},{"name":"Robertson AG"}],"citationCount":37,"date":"2019-11-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics (Oxford, England)","title":"RTNsurvival: an R/Bioconductor package for regulatory network survival analysis."},"pmcid":"PMC6821288","pmid":"30923832"}],"topic":[{"term":"Molecular interactions, pathways and networks","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0602"},{"term":"Transcriptomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3308"}],"validated":1},"bioconductor":{"name":"RTN","version":"2.36.0","license":"Artistic-2.0","summary":"RTN: Reconstruction of Transcriptional regulatory Networks and analysis of regulons","description":"A transcriptional regulatory network (TRN) consists of a collection of transcription factors (TFs) and the regulated target genes. TFs are regulators that recognize specific DNA sequences and guide the expression of the genome, either activating or repressing the expression the target genes. The set of genes controlled by the same TF forms a regulon. This package provides classes and methods for the reconstruction of TRNs and analysis of regulons.","home":"http://dx.doi.org/10.1038/ncomms3464","author":"Clarice Groeneveld [ctb], Gordon Robertson [ctb], Xin Wang [aut], Michael Fletcher [aut], Florian Markowetz [aut], Kerstin Meyer [aut], and Mauro Castro [aut]","maintainer":"Mauro Castro <mauro.a.castro@gmail.com>","bioc_views":["GeneExpression","GeneRegulation","GeneSetEnrichment","GeneticVariability","GraphAndNetwork","Network","NetworkEnrichment","NetworkInference","Software","Transcription"],"git_url":"https://git.bioconductor.org/packages/RTN","last_commit_date":"2026-04-28"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:rtn:1.12.0/cmd/bioconductor.org","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"sourcecode":{"interpreted":true}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"manual":true},"license":{"open_source":true,"osi":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":150,"citations":[{"count":1,"year":-1},{"count":3,"year":2013},{"count":5,"year":2014},{"count":7,"year":2016},{"count":8,"year":2018},{"count":8,"year":2026},{"count":9,"year":2020},{"count":10,"year":2015},{"count":11,"year":2017},{"count":11,"year":2023},{"count":11,"year":2024},{"count":12,"year":2019},{"count":17,"year":2022},{"count":18,"year":2025},{"count":19,"year":2021}],"doi":"10.1038/ncomms3464","pmcid":"PMC3778544","pmid":"24043118","ref_count":55,"refs":[{"count":1,"year":2000},{"count":1,"year":2001},{"count":1,"year":2002},{"count":2,"year":2003},{"count":3,"year":2004},{"count":3,"year":2005},{"count":3,"year":2006},{"count":3,"year":2013},{"count":6,"year":2009},{"count":6,"year":2010},{"count":6,"year":2011},{"count":6,"year":2012},{"count":7,"year":2007},{"count":7,"year":2008}],"title":"Master regulators of FGFR2 signalling and breast cancer risk.","year":2013}]}],"summary":{"description":true},"website":{"bioschemas":false,"copyright":false,"https":true,"license":false,"operational":200,"redirects":[{"status":301,"url":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/RTN.html"}],"ssl":true,"ssl_err":null}},"support":{"email":true}},{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:rtn:1.12.0/lib/bioconductor.org","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotoo